WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.640
Content Security Policy, or CSP, is a resource-loading policy for browsers.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:13.780
It tells the browser which sources are allowed for scripts, styles, images, fonts, frames, connections, forms, and other content.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:21.680
A strong CSP can reduce the impact of injected markup or script because the browser has a separate policy that limits what can load or execute.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:28.460
That makes CSP one of the most powerful security headers, and also one of the easiest to break if it is copied blindly.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:38.760
Common directives include default-src, script-src, style-src, img-src, connect-src, object-src, base-uri, and form-action.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:40.900
default-src can provide a fallback.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.560
script-src controls script sources and is often the most sensitive directive.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:46.700
style-src controls styles.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:49.160
img-src covers image locations.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:54.580
connect-src affects fetch, XHR, WebSocket, and similar connections.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:57.120
object-src can usually be locked down.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:04.220
base-uri and form-action reduce risks around base tag injection and unexpected form submission destinations.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.840
CSP should reflect the real application.

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:15.460
A single-page application that calls APIs, loads analytics, and uses a CDN will need different directives from a static documentation site.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.160
A legacy app with inline scripts may require a staged modernization plan.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:25.660
Nonces and hashes can support safer script execution patterns, but they need build and rendering support.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:30.760
Overly broad values such as unrestricted wildcards often give a false sense of protection.

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:36.820
Report-only mode is useful because it lets teams observe possible policy violations before blocking users.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:45.040
A report-only policy can reveal forgotten resource sources, browser extensions, development tooling, and application paths that need cleanup.

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:48.300
After review, the policy can be tightened and enforced.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.680
CSP is not a one-time header.

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:57.140
It is a maintained control that should be reviewed when frontend architecture, third-party scripts, or deployment patterns change.
